course: dictionary
lesson: 10920
title: Meet the vole
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A vole is a small, plump animal that looks a bit like a mouse. It has a short tail, small ears and a blunt nose, and it lives in fields, woods and by rivers.
explain: Read the context to work out an unknown word. «Owls, foxes, cats, weasels, snakes and hawks all hunt mice, and a family of barn owls can eat over a thousand mice and voles in a year.» Mice and voles are listed together as small creatures that get hunted, so a vole is a small animal like a mouse.
explain: The word after vole often tells you which kind it is. «Field vole», «bank vole» and «water vole» are three different kinds. The water vole is the one that lives by rivers.
fact: A vole is a rodent, a small animal with sharp front teeth for gnawing.
fact: «Voles» is the plural of «vole», so you say one vole and two voles.
fact: Voles are food for owls, kestrels, foxes, weasels and stoats, so they are an important part of food chains.
fact: The water vole is a British riverbank animal that has become rare.
fact: Ratty in «The Wind in the Willows» is really a water vole.
exercise: «A great grey owl can plunge through deep snow to grab a vole it has only heard.» What is a vole in this sentence? | A large bird that lives in snow | *A small animal that owls hunt | A sort of river boat | A kind of deep snow
exercise: «Picture a kestrel over a motorway verge: it faces the wind, flaps fast and holds its head perfectly still while it watches the grass for a vole.» What is the kestrel doing? | *Watching the grass, ready to hunt | Sleeping in the long grass | Building a nest in a tree | Swimming in the river
exercise: Which phrase names the vole that lives by rivers? | barn vole | field vole | bank vole | *water vole
test: Which description fits a vole? | A tall tree with long, thin roots | A large grey bird that hunts at night | A fish with bright fins | *A small, plump animal with a short tail and small ears
test: Ratty in «The Wind in the Willows» is really which animal? | A kestrel | *A water vole | A lemming | A barn owl
exam: «Water voles have vanished from most of the places in Britain where they once lived.» What does vanished mean here? | *Disappeared, and are no longer there | Were found in every river | Grew bigger and stronger | Moved to the next field
